{UTF-8}
ε R=1
	~	Dmnl
	~	Positive lower-bound parameter used in the MAX functions of the Emissions \
		equation to prevent division by zero when Low-carbon consumption or \
		Low-carbon production approaches zero.
	|

Initial cumulative emissions=1
	~	tCO2
	~		|

Ef=2
	~	Dmnl
	~	Relative weight assigned to production-related emissions in the Emissions \
		equation.
	|

Ec=1
	~	Dmnl
	~	Relative weight assigned to consumption-related emissions in the Emissions \
		equation.
	|

Emissions=( Ec/SQRT(MAX ("Low-carbon consumption",ε R)) + Ef/SQRT(MAX("Low-carbon production"\
		,ε R)) ) * E ref
	~	tCO2/Year
	~	Annual emissions flow determined by the consumption and production. Higher \
		values of Low-carbon consumption and Low-carbon production reduce \
		emissions through the inverse relationships in the equation.
	|

T7=5
	~	Year
	~	Time delay from Climate-supportive politicians in power to Low-carbon \
		production, representing the time required for firms to respond to climate \
		policy and regulation.
	|

"Low-carbon production"=Scale parameter * SQRT(SQRT(DELAY1("Climate-supportive politicians in power"\
		, T7)) * SQRT
	(DELAY1("Low-carbon consumption", T8))) * (Dynamic women’s share as firm managers \
		* Women’s impact as firm managers
	 + (1-Dynamic women’s share as firm managers) * 0.5)
	~	Dmnl
	~	This variable represents Firm-manager role. It captures the extent to \
		which firms and managers adopt production practices, technologies, inputs, \
		and strategies that reduce carbon emissions. A larger value indicates more \
		climate-friendly production.
	|

T8=1
	~	Year
	~	Time delay from Low-carbon consumption to Low-carbon production, \
		representing the time required for firms to respond to changes in consumer \
		demand.
	|

"Climate-supportive politicians in power"=Scale parameter * SQRT(DELAY1("Pro-climate voting"\
		, T4)) * (Dynamic women’s share as politicians
	 * Women’s impact as politicians + (1-Dynamic women’s share as politicians) * 0.5\
		)
	~	Dmnl
	~	This variable represents Politician role. It captures the extent to which \
		politicians implement climate-oriented policies and measures. A larger \
		value indicates stronger climate-supportive political action.
	|

T4=2
	~	Year
	~	Time delay from Pro-climate voting to Climate-supportive politicians in \
		power, representing the time required for electoral preferences to \
		translate into political power and policy influence.
	|

T5=1
	~	Year
	~	Time delay from Promotion of low-carbon lifestyles to Low-carbon \
		consumption, representing the time required for lifestyle influencers to \
		affect consumption choices.
	|

T6=2
	~	Year
	~	Time delay from Climate-supportive politicians in power to Low-carbon \
		consumption, representing the time required for climate policy to affect \
		consumer behaviour.
	|

"Low-carbon consumption"=Scale parameter * SQRT(SQRT(DELAY1("Climate-supportive politicians in power"\
		, T6)) * SQRT
	(DELAY1("Promotion of low-carbon lifestyles", T5))) * (Women’s share as consumers \
		* Women’s impact as consumers
	 + (1-Women’s share as consumers) * 0.5)
	~	Dmnl
	~	This variable represents Consumer role. It captures the extent to which \
		individuals adopt consumption patterns with lower carbon impacts, \
		including choices related to goods and services, transport, energy use, \
		and other household activities. A larger value indicates more low-carbon \
		consumption.
	|

"Pro-climate voting"=Scale parameter * SQRT(DELAY1("Promotion of climate-supportive opinions"\
		, T3)) * (Women’s share as voters
	 * Women’s impact as voters + (1-Women’s share as voters) * 0.5)
	~	Dmnl
	~	This variable represents Voter role. It captures the extent to which \
		individuals use their voting behaviour to support politicians and \
		political options that favour stronger climate action. A larger value \
		indicates stronger pro-climate voting.
	|

T3=3
	~	Year
	~	Time delay from Promotion of climate-supportive opinions to Pro-climate \
		voting, representing the time required for opinion influence to affect \
		voting behaviour.
	|

Dynamic women’s share as firm managers=IF THEN ELSE(Time<=15, 0.32,
	IF THEN ELSE(Time<=25, 0.32 + 0.18*(Time-15)/Transition time, 0.5))
	~	Dmnl
	~	This variable represents the share of women in the firm-manager role. It \
		increases exogenously from its initial value toward gender parity (0.5) \
		over years 15–25. The change is used to examine how increasing women’s \
		representation in firm management affects the role variable Low-carbon \
		production.
	|

Dynamic women’s share as opinion influencers=IF THEN ELSE(Time<=10, 0.3 + 0.2*Time/\
		Transition time , 0.5)
	~	Dmnl
	~	This variable represents the share of women in the opinion-influencer \
		role. It increases exogenously from its initial value toward gender parity \
		(0.5) over years 0–10. The change is used to examine how increasing \
		women’s representation as opinion-influencers affects the role variable \
		Promotion of climate-supportive opinions.
	|

Dynamic women’s share as politicians=IF THEN ELSE(Time<=10, 0.28,
	IF THEN ELSE(Time<=20, 0.28 + 0.22*(Time-10)/Transition time, 0.5))
	~	Dmnl
	~	This variable represents the share of women in the politician role. It \
		increases exogenously from its initial value toward gender parity (0.5) \
		over years 10–20. The change is used to examine how increasing women’s \
		representation in politics affects the role variable Climate-supportive \
		politicians in power.
	|

Transition time=10
	~	Year
	~		|

Women’s impact as firm managers=0.7
	~	Dmnl
	~	This parameter represents the average climate-supportive impact of women \
		in firm-management roles on production practices. The corresponding impact \
		of men is fixed at 0.5 in the role equation.
	|

Cumulative emissions= INTEG (Emissions,
		Initial cumulative emissions)
	~	tCO2
	~	Stock variable that accumulates the annual Emissions flow over time, \
		starting from Initial cumulative emissions.
	|

E ref=1
	~	tCO2/Year
	~	Reference emissions level used as a multiplier after the consumption and \
		production terms determine the dimensionless emissions measure. Because \
		carries the unit tCO2/year, multiplying by it expresses the resulting \
		emissions flow in tCO2/year. It does not alter how consumption and \
		production affect emissions; it only sets the unit and scale of the \
		resulting emissions value. It is normalized to 1 because the model focuses \
		on relative rather than empirically calibrated absolute emissions.
	|

Women’s impact as voters=0.7
	~	Dmnl
	~	This parameter represents the average climate-supportive impact of women \
		in the voter role. The corresponding impact of men is fixed at 0.5 in the \
		role equation.
	|

Women’s share as consumers=0.6
	~	Dmnl
	~	Share of the consumer role attributed to women in the model; the \
		corresponding share of men is one minus this value.
	|

Women’s share as lifestyle influencers=0.8
	~	Dmnl
	~	Share of lifestyle influencers attributed to women in the model; the \
		corresponding share of men is one minus this value.
	|

Women’s impact as consumers=0.8
	~	Dmnl
	~	This parameter represents the average tendency of women in the consumer \
		role toward lower-carbon consumption. The corresponding impact of men is \
		fixed at 0.5 in the role equation.
	|

Women’s share as voters=0.53
	~	Dmnl
	~	Share of the voter role attributed to women in the model; the \
		corresponding share of men is one minus this value.
	|

Women’s impact as lifestyle influencers=0.8
	~	Dmnl
	~	This parameter represents the average effectiveness of women as lifestyle \
		influencers in promoting lower-carbon lifestyles. The corresponding impact \
		of men is fixed at 0.5 in the role equation.
	|

Women’s impact as opinion influencers=0.65
	~	Dmnl
	~	This parameter represens the average effectiveness of women as opinion \
		influencers in promoting climate-supportive voting. The corresponding \
		impact of men is fixed at 0.5 in the role equation.
	|

Women’s impact as politicians=0.75
	~	Dmnl
	~	This parameter represents the average climate-supportive impact of women \
		in political roles. The corresponding impact of men is fixed at 0.5 in the \
		role equation.
	|

"Promotion of low-carbon lifestyles"=Scale parameter * (Women’s share as lifestyle influencers\
		 * Women’s impact as lifestyle influencers + (1-Women’s share as lifestyle influencers\
		) * 0.5)
	~	Dmnl
	~	This variable represents Lifestyle influencer role. It captures the extent \
		to which individuals influence others to adopt more sustainable and \
		lower-carbon lifestyles and consumption patterns. A larger value indicates \
		stronger promotion of low-carbon lifestyles.
	|

"Promotion of climate-supportive opinions"=Scale parameter * (Dynamic women’s share as opinion influencers\
		 * Women’s impact as opinion influencers + (1-Dynamic women’s share as opinion influencers\
		) * 0.5)
	~	Dmnl
	~	This variable represents Opinion influencer role. It captures the extent \
		to which individuals influence others’ voting behaviour and encourage \
		support for climate-oriented politicians. A larger value indicates \
		stronger climate-supportive influence.
	|

Scale parameter=20
	~	Dmnl
	~	Dimensionless scaling parameter applied uniformly to all role variables to \
		keep their magnitudes comparable and maintain model stability.
	|

********************************************************
	.Control
********************************************************~
		Simulation Control Parameters
	|

FINAL TIME  = 50
	~	Year
	~	The final time for the simulation.
	|

INITIAL TIME  = 0
	~	Year
	~	The initial time for the simulation.
	|

SAVEPER  = 
        TIME STEP
	~	Year [0,?]
	~	The frequency with which output is stored.
	|

TIME STEP  = 1
	~	Year [0,?]
	~	The time step for the simulation.
	|

